[candidate · 5:01] That lines up cleanly — 58 + 22 + 14 = 94, so 6% profit; and 42 + 22 + 12 = 76, so 24%. Good, the lines reconcile.

COGS is basically the whole story: 16 of the 18 points. Overhead adds 2, labor adds nothing.

In dollar terms, indexing old revenue to 100: COGS was 42. Today it's 58% of 115, call it 115 × 0.58 ≈ 67. So COGS grew about 25 on a base of 42 — roughly 60% growth against 15% revenue growth. Estimating, but that's the fire.

So the question is why COGS per dollar of revenue jumped. Two broad buckets: input cost per unit went up — bean prices, dairy, cups, freight — or they're selling more volume at prices that haven't moved, i.e. they absorbed input inflation without repricing. Waste or spoilage is a third.

