[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 11:15] I don't have competitor pricing or elasticity data. Beyond repricing, what else could the client do to reverse the margin decline? Give me a structured set of options.

[candidate · 12:45] No elasticity data, so I'll assume specialty coffee is relatively inelastic given it's a habitual, low-ticket purchase, and flag that as the key thing to test.

Three buckets beyond straight repricing.

First, reduce input cost per unit. Lock in supply with forward contracts or hedging on beans so they're not fully exposed to spot. Consolidate purchasing across all 200 stores for scale leverage — at that size they should have real buying power. Re-tender dairy and packaging suppliers, and look at cheaper packaging specs.

Second, reduce input volume per cup. Portion control, waste and spoilage tracking, and recipe standardization. If they're over-pouring milk, that's free margin.

Third, shift mix rather than list price. Push high-margin items — food, which has fewer coffee inputs, or larger sizes and add-ons where the incremental input cost is small. Also loyalty programs to lift ticket size without an obvious price rise.

Fourth, overhead: it's only about 2 points, roughly 2.3 on a 115 revenue base, but it's worth a look since corporate costs grew faster than stores.
