[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 12:48] Those split cost-side and mix-side. On the revenue side, what levers haven't you named, and which of this whole set would you lead with?

[candidate · 14:18] On revenue I mostly named mix, so what's missing: volume and traffic levers — new dayparts like afternoon or food-led traffic, extending hours, subscriptions or prepaid cards to lock in frequency. Channel expansion: delivery and mobile order, plus selling retail bags of beans, which carries the bean cost but almost no labor. And store footprint — they've got 200 locations, so new units or relocating underperformers. Plus pricing architecture as distinct from list price: sizes, tiers, and cutting discounts or promos if any exist.

But I'd lead with repricing, clearly. The math forces it: inputs are up roughly 38% blended and price is up 0%. To restore COGS to 42% of revenue with today's cost base, revenue needs to rise by about 67/0.42 ≈ 160 versus 115 today — call it a 38% price increase, which is obviously too much at once.

So I'd lead with a staged increase of maybe 8–10%, which on a 115 base is roughly 10 points of revenue dropping almost entirely to profit — that alone roughly doubles margin. Then layer procurement and waste on top.
