[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 14:21] Pull it together for the CEO. What's your recommendation, and what are the risks?

[candidate · 15:51] Recommendation: Brew & Bean should reprice, in stages, starting now — that's the root cause and the primary fix.

The diagnosis: margin fell 18 points, and 16 of those sit in COGS, which went from 42% to 58% of revenue. Beans are up 40%, everything else — dairy, packaging, food — up 37.5%. I rebuilt COGS from those two inputs plus volume and landed at 66 against an actual 67, so inflation explains essentially all of it. Labor held flat, so this isn't an operations failure. Prices simply haven't moved in two years against roughly 38% input inflation.

The plan: lead with an 8–10% price rise, phased, which on a 115 revenue base is about 10 points falling almost entirely to profit — roughly doubling margin to 12–13%. Then layer procurement — hedge beans, consolidate purchasing across 200 stores — plus waste and portion control, and mix toward food and retail bags. Overhead's worth 2 points; smaller, but take it.

Risks: no elasticity data, so volume response is unknown — I'd pilot in a subset of markets first. Competitors may not have repriced either, which would expose us. And further input inflation could outrun the increase, so I'd build in a repricing cadence rather than treating this as one-off.

Next steps: pilot pricing in 20 stores, measure traffic, and run a procurement benchmark in parallel.
